# KNOWN GAP — BOTH LANES. `.map()` with an INLINE CLOSURE that changes the
# element type mints its accumulator from the INPUT element type, so a `String`
# result comes back out as a raw pointer at rc 0.
#
#     Vector[int] nums = [3, 4]
#     auto v = nums.map((int n): mk("t", "ag"))
#     print(v.get(0).unwrap())    # prints e.g. 187651356173072, not "tag"
#
# ⚠ THE COMPREHENSION EQUIVALENT IS CORRECT (prints "tag"):
#
#     auto v = [mk("t", "ag") for n in nums]
#
# and that is the whole point: this is the SAME mint class R44 Track A fixed at
# the comprehension producer, surviving at a SIBLING producer
# (`try_lower_vector_hof`, whose own comment admits it mints from the input
# element type). Core #4 -- fix the CLASS, not the instance: a producer-level
# fix that leaves a sibling producer minting from the wrong channel has fixed
# one site, not the class.
#
# ⚠ Named functions are FINE -- an earlier round gated cross-type `map` on
# `fn_sigs` return types for named callees only, so the INLINE CLOSURE is the
# discriminator, not `map` itself.
#
# INTENDED (asserted below): prints 2 then tag.
#
# This fixture pays the "No corpus fixture exercises it today" debt recorded on
# the TODO entry, which had no durable repro. Measured 2026-08-24 by the R44
# Track-K brief-review pass 7, orchestrator-verified: the entry called this
# LATENT, but it reproduces ACTIVE at rc 0 with garbage output on both lanes.

String mk(String a, String b):
    return a + b

void main():
    Vector[int] nums = [3, 4]
    auto v = nums.map((int n): mk("t", "ag"))
    print(v.len())
    print(v.get(0).unwrap())
